766-144485-0126 V:0.4995 A:0.3809 D:0.4621 THERE WAS A SOFTENED SHADE UPON HER FACE THROWN FROM SOME GREEN LEAVES OVERHANGING THE OPEN WINDOW OF THE LOW ROOM AND I THOUGHT ALL THE WAY TO DOCTORS COMMONS OF THE NIGHT WHEN I HAD SEEN IT LOOKING AT HIM AS HE READ
766-144485-0127 V:0.4638 A:0.3798 D:0.4638 I WAS PRETTY BUSY NOW UP AT FIVE IN THE MORNING AND HOME AT NINE OR TEN AT NIGHT
766-144485-0128 V:0.5955 A:0.4409 D:0.5454 BUT I HAD INFINITE SATISFACTION IN BEING SO CLOSELY ENGAGED AND NEVER WALKED SLOWLY ON ANY ACCOUNT AND FELT ENTHUSIASTICALLY THAT THE MORE I TIRED MYSELF THE MORE I WAS DOING TO DESERVE DORA
766-144485-0129 V:0.4707 A:0.4083 D:0.5065 I HAD NOT REVEALED MYSELF IN MY ALTERED CHARACTER TO DORA YET BECAUSE SHE WAS COMING TO SEE MISS MILLS IN A FEW DAYS AND I DEFERRED ALL I HAD TO TELL HER UNTIL THEN MERELY INFORMING HER IN MY LETTERS ALL OUR COMMUNICATIONS WERE SECRETLY FORWARDED THROUGH MISS MILLS THAT I HAD MUCH TO TELL HER
766-144485-0130 V:0.4911 A:0.4290 D:0.5377 IN THE MEANTIME I PUT MYSELF ON A SHORT ALLOWANCE OF BEARS GREASE WHOLLY ABANDONED SCENTED SOAP AND LAVENDER WATER AND SOLD OFF THREE WAISTCOATS AT A PRODIGIOUS SACRIFICE AS BEING TOO LUXURIOUS FOR MY STERN CAREER
766-144485-0131 V:0.4547 A:0.4213 D:0.5174 NOT SATISFIED WITH ALL THESE PROCEEDINGS BUT BURNING WITH IMPATIENCE TO DO SOMETHING MORE I WENT TO SEE TRADDLES NOW LODGING UP BEHIND THE PARAPET OF A HOUSE IN CASTLE STREET HOLBORN
766-144485-0132 V:0.5034 A:0.3880 D:0.4909 MISTER DICK WHO HAD BEEN WITH ME TO HIGHGATE TWICE ALREADY AND HAD RESUMED HIS COMPANIONSHIP WITH THE DOCTOR I TOOK WITH ME
766-144485-0133 V:0.3438 A:0.4129 D:0.5200 I TOOK MISTER DICK WITH ME BECAUSE ACUTELY SENSITIVE TO MY AUNTS REVERSES AND SINCERELY BELIEVING THAT NO GALLEY SLAVE OR CONVICT WORKED AS I DID HE HAD BEGUN TO FRET AND WORRY HIMSELF OUT OF SPIRITS AND APPETITE AS HAVING NOTHING USEFUL TO DO
766-144485-0134 V:0.4219 A:0.4120 D:0.5296 IN THIS CONDITION HE FELT MORE INCAPABLE OF FINISHING THE MEMORIAL THAN EVER AND THE HARDER HE WORKED AT IT THE OFTENER THAT UNLUCKY HEAD OF KING CHARLES THE FIRST GOT INTO IT
766-144485-0135 V:0.4262 A:0.3785 D:0.4905 SERIOUSLY APPREHENDING THAT HIS MALADY WOULD INCREASE UNLESS WE PUT SOME INNOCENT DECEPTION UPON HIM AND CAUSED HIM TO BELIEVE THAT HE WAS USEFUL OR UNLESS WE COULD PUT HIM IN THE WAY OF BEING REALLY USEFUL WHICH WOULD BE BETTER I MADE UP MY MIND TO TRY IF TRADDLES COULD HELP US
766-144485-0136 V:0.5713 A:0.3832 D:0.4929 BEFORE WE WENT I WROTE TRADDLES A FULL STATEMENT OF ALL THAT HAD HAPPENED AND TRADDLES WROTE ME BACK A CAPITAL ANSWER EXPRESSIVE OF HIS SYMPATHY AND FRIENDSHIP
766-144485-0137 V:0.5484 A:0.4097 D:0.4934 WE FOUND HIM HARD AT WORK WITH HIS INKSTAND AND PAPERS REFRESHED BY THE SIGHT OF THE FLOWER POT STAND AND THE LITTLE ROUND TABLE IN A CORNER OF THE SMALL APARTMENT
766-144485-0138 V:0.6758 A:0.4184 D:0.5407 HE RECEIVED US CORDIALLY AND MADE FRIENDS WITH MISTER DICK IN A MOMENT
766-144485-0139 V:0.4771 A:0.4241 D:0.5178 MISTER DICK PROFESSED AN ABSOLUTE CERTAINTY OF HAVING SEEN HIM BEFORE AND WE BOTH SAID VERY LIKELY
766-144485-0140 V:0.4992 A:0.4004 D:0.5059 THE FIRST SUBJECT ON WHICH I HAD TO CONSULT TRADDLES WAS THIS I HAD HEARD THAT MANY MEN DISTINGUISHED IN VARIOUS PURSUITS HAD BEGUN LIFE BY REPORTING THE DEBATES IN PARLIAMENT
766-144485-0141 V:0.5421 A:0.3898 D:0.4938 TRADDLES HAVING MENTIONED NEWSPAPERS TO ME AS ONE OF HIS HOPES I HAD PUT THE TWO THINGS TOGETHER AND TOLD TRADDLES IN MY LETTER THAT I WISHED TO KNOW HOW I COULD QUALIFY MYSELF FOR THIS PURSUIT
766-144485-0142 V:0.5835 A:0.4061 D:0.5271 THAT THE MERE MECHANICAL ACQUISITION NECESSARY EXCEPT IN RARE CASES FOR THOROUGH EXCELLENCE IN IT THAT IS TO SAY A PERFECT AND ENTIRE COMMAND OF THE MYSTERY OF SHORT HAND WRITING AND READING WAS ABOUT EQUAL IN DIFFICULTY TO THE MASTERY OF SIX LANGUAGES
766-144485-0143 V:0.5613 A:0.4412 D:0.5396 TRADDLES REASONABLY SUPPOSED THAT THIS WOULD SETTLE THE BUSINESS BUT I ONLY FEELING THAT HERE INDEED WERE A FEW TALL TREES TO BE HEWN DOWN IMMEDIATELY RESOLVED TO WORK MY WAY ON TO DORA THROUGH THIS THICKET AXE IN HAND I AM VERY MUCH OBLIGED TO YOU MY DEAR TRADDLES SAID I
766-144485-0144 V:0.5829 A:0.4868 D:0.5858 ILL BEGIN TOMORROW TRADDLES LOOKED ASTONISHED AS HE WELL MIGHT BUT HE HAD NO NOTION AS YET OF MY RAPTUROUS CONDITION
766-144485-0148 V:0.4692 A:0.3924 D:0.4930 I PASSED THAT OFF AND BROUGHT MISTER DICK ON THE CARPET
766-144485-0149 V:0.3534 A:0.4586 D:0.5407 YOU SEE SAID MISTER DICK WISTFULLY IF I COULD EXERT MYSELF MISTER TRADDLES IF I COULD BEAT A DRUM OR BLOW ANYTHING POOR FELLOW
766-144485-0150 V:0.4313 A:0.4244 D:0.5349 I HAVE LITTLE DOUBT HE WOULD HAVE PREFERRED SUCH AN EMPLOYMENT IN HIS HEART TO ALL OTHERS
766-144485-0151 V:0.5817 A:0.4599 D:0.5594 TRADDLES WHO WOULD NOT HAVE SMILED FOR THE WORLD REPLIED COMPOSEDLY BUT YOU ARE A VERY GOOD PENMAN SIR
766-144485-0154 V:0.4601 A:0.4714 D:0.5440 DONT YOU THINK SAID TRADDLES YOU COULD COPY WRITINGS SIR IF I GOT THEM FOR YOU MISTER DICK LOOKED DOUBTFULLY AT ME
766-144485-0155 V:0.3409 A:0.4281 D:0.4989 EH TROTWOOD I SHOOK MY HEAD
766-144485-0156 V:0.3280 A:0.3451 D:0.4549 MISTER DICK SHOOK HIS AND SIGHED
766-144485-0157 V:0.5108 A:0.3740 D:0.4732 TELL HIM ABOUT THE MEMORIAL SAID MISTER DICK
766-144485-0158 V:0.3724 A:0.4250 D:0.5281 I EXPLAINED TO TRADDLES THAT THERE WAS A DIFFICULTY IN KEEPING KING CHARLES THE FIRST OUT OF MISTER DICKS MANUSCRIPTS MISTER DICK IN THE MEANWHILE LOOKING VERY DEFERENTIALLY AND SERIOUSLY AT TRADDLES AND SUCKING HIS THUMB
766-144485-0159 V:0.5438 A:0.4462 D:0.5423 BUT THESE WRITINGS YOU KNOW THAT I SPEAK OF ARE ALREADY DRAWN UP AND FINISHED SAID TRADDLES AFTER A LITTLE CONSIDERATION MISTER
766-144485-0160 V:0.2375 A:0.4644 D:0.5690 DICK HAS NOTHING TO DO WITH THEM
766-144485-0161 V:0.4328 A:0.4506 D:0.5212 WOULDNT THAT MAKE A DIFFERENCE COPPERFIELD
766-144485-0164 V:0.5590 A:0.3936 D:0.4897 ON A TABLE BY THE WINDOW IN BUCKINGHAM STREET WE SET OUT THE WORK TRADDLES PROCURED FOR HIM WHICH WAS TO MAKE I FORGET HOW MANY COPIES OF A LEGAL DOCUMENT ABOUT SOME RIGHT OF WAY AND ON ANOTHER TABLE WE SPREAD THE LAST UNFINISHED ORIGINAL OF THE GREAT MEMORIAL
766-144485-0165 V:0.4931 A:0.4130 D:0.5189 OUR INSTRUCTIONS TO MISTER DICK WERE THAT HE SHOULD COPY EXACTLY WHAT HE HAD BEFORE HIM WITHOUT THE LEAST DEPARTURE FROM THE ORIGINAL AND THAT WHEN HE FELT IT NECESSARY TO MAKE THE SLIGHTEST ALLUSION TO KING CHARLES THE FIRST HE SHOULD FLY TO THE MEMORIAL
766-144485-0166 V:0.4176 A:0.3971 D:0.5012 WE EXHORTED HIM TO BE RESOLUTE IN THIS AND LEFT MY AUNT TO OBSERVE HIM
766-144485-0167 V:0.4531 A:0.3704 D:0.4657 MY AUNT REPORTED TO US AFTERWARDS THAT AT FIRST HE WAS LIKE A MAN PLAYING THE KETTLE DRUMS AND CONSTANTLY DIVIDED HIS ATTENTIONS BETWEEN THE TWO BUT THAT FINDING THIS CONFUSE AND FATIGUE HIM AND HAVING HIS COPY THERE PLAINLY BEFORE HIS EYES HE SOON SAT AT IT IN AN ORDERLY BUSINESS LIKE MANNER AND POSTPONED THE MEMORIAL TO A MORE CONVENIENT TIME
766-144485-0168 V:0.4876 A:0.3991 D:0.4957 IN A WORD ALTHOUGH WE TOOK GREAT CARE THAT HE SHOULD HAVE NO MORE TO DO THAN WAS GOOD FOR HIM AND ALTHOUGH HE DID NOT BEGIN WITH THE BEGINNING OF A WEEK HE EARNED BY THE FOLLOWING SATURDAY NIGHT TEN SHILLINGS AND NINE PENCE TO ALL THE SHOPS IN THE NEIGHBOURHOOD TO CHANGE THIS TREASURE INTO SIXPENCES OR HIS BRINGING THEM TO MY AUNT ARRANGED IN THE FORM OF A HEART UPON A WAITER WITH TEARS OF JOY AND PRIDE IN HIS EYES
766-144485-0169 V:0.6919 A:0.4566 D:0.5571 HE WAS LIKE ONE UNDER THE PROPITIOUS INFLUENCE OF A CHARM FROM THE MOMENT OF HIS BEING USEFULLY EMPLOYED AND IF THERE WERE A HAPPY MAN IN THE WORLD THAT SATURDAY NIGHT IT WAS THE GRATEFUL CREATURE WHO THOUGHT MY AUNT THE MOST WONDERFUL WOMAN IN EXISTENCE AND ME THE MOST WONDERFUL YOUNG MAN
766-144485-0170 V:0.4567 A:0.4892 D:0.5641 NO STARVING NOW TROTWOOD SAID MISTER DICK SHAKING HANDS WITH ME IN A CORNER ILL PROVIDE FOR HER SIR AND HE FLOURISHED HIS TEN FINGERS IN THE AIR AS IF THEY WERE TEN BANKS
766-144485-0171 V:0.4285 A:0.4360 D:0.5243 I HARDLY KNOW WHICH WAS THE BETTER PLEASED TRADDLES OR I
